---
export const title = "Supercar";
export const desc = "Wedge shaped and endlessly driving";
export const jsFile = "supercar.js";
---

<style>
  rex-supercar {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 60rem;
  }
</style>

<h1>{title}</h1>

<div class="desc">
  An isometric Ferrari Testarossa driving along an endless city highway, with traffic, overhead signs,
  tower blocks, retro video game billboards and a sidewalk with a few food vendors, bus stops, parking meters and pedestrians, and a car park beside it. The whole scene is built from CSS 3D transformed elements and a little JS, no image files.
  <br /><br />
</div>

<div class="demo-wrap">
  <rex-supercar></rex-supercar>
</div>
<h3>In Code</h3>
<div class="code-wrap">
  <pre><code class="usage language-javascript">
    {`
  // import the component
  import "../lib/supercar";

  //use it in your HTML
  <rex-supercar speed="320" color="#f2f2f6"></rex-supercar>

  //attributes
  //  speed  - how fast the road scrolls (default 320)
  //  color  - body colour (default #f2f2f6, white; #d4001a is Rosso Corsa red)
  //  paused - freezes the animation
  `}
  </code></pre>
  <br />
  <section class="highlight">
    <h3>How it works</h3>
    The world is a flat plane tilted with <code>rotateX(55deg) rotateZ(45deg)</code> to give the isometric view. The car
    is built from tilted panels and cuboids placed in that 3D space. The road never moves; its surface, lane dashes
    and guardrail posts are repeating textures shifted by one period at a time so the loop is seamless, and the lamps,
    buildings, traffic, signs and billboards travel round a ring. The billboard posters are pixel art painted onto small canvases at start up.
  </section>
</div>

<script>
  import "../lib/supercar";
</script>
